    "OtherAbstract": {
      "Type": "PIP",
      "Language": "eng",
      "AbstractText": "Although enormous health improvements have been made in South Africa in recent times, the gap in public health indices between whites and urban blacks is still far wider than is hoped for. Infant mortality rate of blacks in Johannesburg is currently 40.7/1000 live births, a figure obtained for urban whites 40 to 50 years ago. As to the gap in time between whites and urban blacks with respect to degenerative diseases such as coronary heart disease (CHD), it is known that CHD is a rarity in urban blacks (CHD is virtually absent in rural blacks). In white populations, CDH was rare during the late 1920's, being responsible for less than 1% of all deaths. Currently however, CHD is the main cause in over a third of all deaths in some parts of the world (New York, 38%; Tel Aviv, 37%)."
    }
